<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:16:Real Mode">
<!--
    kuna #1288: an x86 real-mode near branch wraps IP inside its code segment,
    and a real-mode address is the linear CS*16 + IP, where CS need not be a
    multiple of 0x1000. kuna's GH-7418 fix (tests/stages/
    gh7418-x86-call-rel16-mask.xml) masked every rel16 target to its low 16
    bits, which is right only for CS 0, so a DOS program loaded at segment
    0x1000 lost all but 12 of its 330 functions. The linear sum is right only
    when IP does not wrap.

    16-bit x86 decodes with x86-16.sla, which keeps CS in the context variable
    realCS (default 0). A far CALL states its target's CS; 0x2b58 is the main
    code segment of that program:

        00100  e8 00 90        CALL 0x9103   ; CS 0, IP 0x0103 - 0x7000 wraps
        40000  9a f8 00 58 2b  CALLF 2b58:00f8 (0x2b678)
        40005  9a f8 00 58 4b  CALLF 4b58:00f8 (0x4b678)
        2b678  e8 ae 93        CALL 0x34a29  ; CS 0x2b58, IP 0x00fb - 0x6c52 wraps
        4b678  e8 ae 93        CALL 0x54a29  ; CS 0x4b58, the same wrap
        5b680  e8 8d 49        CALL 0x60010  ; CS 0 cannot hold 0x5b680: linear sum

    Stage: P1/P2 decode (x86 SLEIGH spec fix, no option). Without realCS the
    mask decodes 0x4a29 for both wrapped calls, the linear sum 0x24a29 and
    0x44a29, and the linear sum also decodes the first call to -0x6efd. A near
    CALL pushes IP inside its segment (0x00fb), not the low half of its linear
    address (0xb67b).
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100">
e80090c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x9103">
b80700c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x2b678">
e8ae93c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x34a29">
b80700c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x40000">
9af800582b9af800584bcb
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x4b678">
e8ae93cb
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x54a29">
b80700c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x5b680">
e88d49c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x60010">
b80700c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100" name="com_main"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x9103" name="com_helper"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x2b678" name="f"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x34a29" name="g"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x40000" name="far_caller"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x4b678" name="k"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x54a29" name="m"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x5b680" name="p"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x60010" name="q"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>disassemble 0x100 0x104</com>
  <com>disassemble 0x5b680 0x5b684</com>
  <com>lo fu com_main</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu far_caller</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>disassemble 0x2b678 0x2b67c</com>
  <com>disassemble 0x4b678 0x4b67c</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu k</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="real-rel16 #1: with CS 0 a CALL at 0x100 wraps IP below 0 to 0x9103" min="1" max="1">^0x00000100: CALL\s+0x9103$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="real-rel16 #2: in CS 0x2b58, which a far CALL states, a CALL at 0x2b678 wraps IP inside its segment to 0x34a29" min="1" max="1">^0x0002b678: CALL\s+0x34a29$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="real-rel16 #3: the same CALL in CS 0x4b58 wraps to 0x54a29" min="1" max="1">^0x0004b678: CALL\s+0x54a29$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="real-rel16 #4: no wrapped call lands in the first 64 KiB (mask) or 64 KiB short (linear sum)" min="0" max="0">CALL\s+(0x4a29|0x24a29|0x44a29|-0x6efd)$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="real-rel16 #5: an instruction CS 0 cannot hold takes the linear sum (0x5b680 to 0x60010)" min="1" max="1">^0x0005b680: CALL\s+0x60010$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="real-rel16 #6: com_main, f and k call what they wrap to" min="3" max="3">^  (com_helper|g|m)\(\);$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="real-rel16 #7: the CALLs in f and k push IP 0x00fb, not the linear low half 0xb67b" min="2" max="2">= 0xfb;$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="real-rel16 #8: no CALL pushes the linear low half" min="0" max="0">= 0xb67b;$</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
